  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1030 PM MDT Mon Aug 24 2026\n\nThe heat dome will sit right over southern New Mexico on Tuesday\nand that will send our high temperatures 10 to 13 degrees above \naverage. Almost all of the locations in the lowlands will have \ntriple digit heat and high temperatures at or a little above 105 \nwill be possible. I have expanded the heat advisory to also now \ninclude most of Dona Ana and most of Luna Counties along with El \nPaso County and the Rio Grande Valley of Hudspeth County. The \nmountains will have just enough lift and moisture to make a few \nthunderstorms possible Tuesday afternoon, though my confidence in \nany rain reaching the ground is low. For Wednesday the upper \nlevel ridge will drift a little to the west, but not far enough to\nkeep us from having another afternoon of record or near record \nhigh temperatures. \n\nFor Thursday and Friday the upper level ridge will be centered\nalong the NM/AZ border. This will allow a few degrees to drop off\nour high temperatures and we will see some scattered afternoon and\nevening thunderstorms, mainly in area mountains, but the lowlands\ncould see a few storms as well. For those hoping to catch a\nview of the lunar eclipse on Thursday night, we will have some \nclouds but we should also have plenty of clear spots to see the \npartial eclipse of the moon.\n\nFor Saturday and Sunday the models are beginning to hint at a\npattern change with the upper level ridge weakening and moving off\nto the east. This would continue the slow downward trend in our \nhigh temperatures (but still well above average) and it will allow\na few more thunderstorms to develop in the lowlands both \nafternoons. For the start of next week, both the extended GFS and \nECMWF indicate that we will have a brief monsoon flow develop \nacross the region, which would increase our rain chances and \ndecrease (by a few degrees) our afternoon high temperatures.",
